Topic 3: Building Models and URL Routing
Now we’ll learn how to use Django Models (for database tables) and connect them with URLs.
________________________________________
 Step 1: What is a Model?
•	A Model in Django is a Python class that represents a database table.
•	Each attribute = column in the database.
•	Django uses ORM (Object Relational Mapper) → means we can write Python code instead of SQL queries.
________________________________________
Step 2: Define a Model
Open blog/models.py and add:
from django.db import models

class Post(models.Model):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)
    content = models.TextField()
    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)

    def __str__(self):
        return self.title
 Explanation:
•	CharField → short text field (title).
•	TextField → large text (content).
•	DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True) → automatically stores creation date.
•	__str__ → shows title in admin panel instead of object ID.
________________________________________
 Step 3: Activate the Model (Migrations)
1.	Tell Django about changes:
2.	python manage.py makemigrations
3.	Apply changes to database:
4.	python manage.py migrate
 Now Django creates a table blog_post in the database.
________________________________________
 Step 4: Add Model to Admin Panel
Open blog/admin.py:
from django.contrib import admin
from .models import Post

admin.site.register(Post)
Now when you go to http://127.0.0.1:8000/admin/ → you’ll see Post model.
(But you need a superuser first:)
python manage.py createsuperuser
Enter username, email, password. Then log in.
________________________________________
 Step 5: Create a View to Show Posts
Open blog/views.py:
from django.http import HttpResponse
from .models import Post

def post_list(request):
    posts = Post.objects.all()
    output = ", ".join([p.title for p in posts])
    return HttpResponse(output)
________________________________________
 Step 6: Add URL Routing
In blog/urls.py:
from django.urls import path
from . import views

urlpatterns = [
    path('posts/', views.post_list, name='post_list'),
]
And in mysite/urls.py (already included earlier, so no change needed).
________________________________________
 Step 7: Test in Browser
1.	Open admin → create a few posts.
2.	Go to → http://127.0.0.1:8000/posts/
 You’ll see a list of post titles separated by commas.
________________________________________
 Summary for Topic 3:
•	Created a Post model (title, content, created_at).
•	Ran migrations → table created in DB.
•	Registered in Admin panel.
•	Created a view to display posts.
