Topic 4: Django Views, Templates, and Forms
So far, we’ve used views with HttpResponse to return plain text.
Now we’ll learn:
1.	Views → Handle logic
2.	Templates → Render dynamic HTML
3.	Forms → Collect user input
________________________________________
Step 1: Create a Template Folder
Inside the blog app, create a templates directory:
blog/
│── templates/
│   │── blog/
│       │── post_list.html
 Important: inside templates/, we create another folder named blog/ to match the app name. This avoids conflicts if multiple apps have templates.
________________________________________
 Step 2: Create Template File
blog/templates/blog/post_list.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Blog</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Posts</h1>
    <ul>
        {% for post in posts %}
            <li><strong>{{ post.title }}</strong> - {{ post.content }}</li>
        {% empty %}
            <li>No posts available.</li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ul>
</body>
</html>
 Explanation:
•	{% for post in posts %} → loops through posts.
•	{{ post.title }} → prints data.
•	{% empty %} → if no posts exist.
________________________________________
 Step 3: Update the View
blog/views.py
from django.shortcuts import render
from .models import Post

def post_list(request):
    posts = Post.objects.all()
    return render(request, 'blog/post_list.html', {'posts': posts})
 Now we’re rendering an HTML template instead of plain text.
________________________________________
 Step 4: Update URL
Already in blog/urls.py:
urlpatterns = [
    path('posts/', views.post_list, name='post_list'),
]
No change needed.
________________________________________
 Step 5: Test in Browser
1.	Add some posts from admin panel.
2.	Visit → http://127.0.0.1:8000/posts/
3.	Now posts are shown inside a styled HTML list 
________________________________________
Step 6: Simple Form Example
Let’s create a form to add posts.
blog/forms.py (create this new file):
from django import forms
from .models import Post

class PostForm(forms.ModelForm):
    class Meta:
        model = Post
        fields = ['title', 'content']
________________________________________
 Add Form View
blog/views.py
from django.shortcuts import render, redirect
from .forms import PostForm

def add_post(request):
    if request.method == "POST":
        form = PostForm(request.POST)
        if form.is_valid():
            form.save()
            return redirect('post_list')
    else:
        form = PostForm()
    return render(request, 'blog/add_post.html', {'form': form})
________________________________________
 Create Template for Form
blog/templates/blog/add_post.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Add Post</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Add a New Post</h1>
    <form method="post">
        {% csrf_token %}
        {{ form.as_p }}
        <button type="submit">Save</button>
    </form>
    <a href="{% url 'post_list' %}">Back to posts</a>
</body>
</html>
________________________________________
 Add URL for Form
blog/urls.py
urlpatterns = [
    path('posts/', views.post_list, name='post_list'),
    path('add/', views.add_post, name='add_post'),
]
________________________________________
 Test It
1.	Visit → http://127.0.0.1:8000/add/
2.	Fill the form → click Save.
3.	Redirects to post list with new post visible 
________________________________________
Summary for Topic 4:
•	Learned Views → Templates → Forms.
•	Moved from plain HttpResponse to HTML templates.
•	Created a form to add posts dynamically.
