Topic 5: Creating and Handling Views for User Interactions
So far we:
•	Listed posts (post_list)
•	Added posts (add_post)
Now we’ll make our app interactive by adding:
1.	Detail View → See single post
2.	Edit Post View
3.	Delete Post View
________________________________________
 Step 1: Detail View
blog/views.py
from django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404

def post_detail(request, pk):
    post = get_object_or_404(Post, pk=pk)
    return render(request, 'blog/post_detail.html', {'post': post})
blog/templates/blog/post_detail.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>{{ post.title }}</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>{{ post.title }}</h1>
    <p>{{ post.content }}</p>
    <small>Created at: {{ post.created_at }}</small><br><br>

    <a href="{% url 'post_list' %}">Back to posts</a> |
    <a href="{% url 'edit_post' post.pk %}">Edit</a> |
    <a href="{% url 'delete_post' post.pk %}">Delete</a>
</body>
</html>
blog/urls.py
urlpatterns = [
    path('posts/', views.post_list, name='post_list'),
    path('add/', views.add_post, name='add_post'),
    path('post/<int:pk>/', views.post_detail, name='post_detail'),
]
 Now clicking on a post will show its details.
________________________________________
 Step 2: Edit Post
blog/views.py
def edit_post(request, pk):
    post = get_object_or_404(Post, pk=pk)
    if request.method == "POST":
        form = PostForm(request.POST, instance=post)
        if form.is_valid():
            form.save()
            return redirect('post_detail', pk=post.pk)
    else:
        form = PostForm(instance=post)
    return render(request, 'blog/edit_post.html', {'form': form})
blog/templates/blog/edit_post.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Edit Post</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Edit Post</h1>
    <form method="post">
        {% csrf_token %}
        {{ form.as_p }}
        <button type="submit">Update</button>
    </form>
    <a href="{% url 'post_detail' form.instance.pk %}">Cancel</a>
</body>
</html>
blog/urls.py
path('post/<int:pk>/edit/', views.edit_post, name='edit_post'),
________________________________________
 Step 3: Delete Post
blog/views.py
def delete_post(request, pk):
    post = get_object_or_404(Post, pk=pk)
    if request.method == "POST":
        post.delete()
        return redirect('post_list')
    return render(request, 'blog/delete_post.html', {'post': post})
blog/templates/blog/delete_post.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Delete Post</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Are you sure you want to delete "{{ post.title }}"?</h1>
    <form method="post">
        {% csrf_token %}
        <button type="submit">Yes, delete</button>
    </form>
    <a href="{% url 'post_detail' post.pk %}">Cancel</a>
</body>
</html>
blog/urls.py
path('post/<int:pk>/delete/', views.delete_post, name='delete_post'),
________________________________________
 Step 4: Update Post List with Links
Update blog/post_list.html:
<ul>
    {% for post in posts %}
        <li>
            <a href="{% url 'post_detail' post.pk %}">{{ post.title }}</a>
        </li>
    {% empty %}
        <li>No posts available.</li>
    {% endfor %}
</ul>
<a href="{% url 'add_post' %}">Add New Post</a>
________________________________________
 Step 5: Test in Browser
1.	Go to http://127.0.0.1:8000/posts/
2.	Click a post → see details
3.	Try Edit → update post
4.	Try Delete → confirm deletion
________________________________________
 Summary for Topic 5
•	Created detail view → view individual post.
•	Added edit view → update posts.
•	Added delete view → remove posts.
•	Connected views with templates and URLs.
•	Now app supports full CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete). 
