Topic 6: Working with Django Templates for Dynamic Content
So far we’ve used basic templates.
Now we’ll explore dynamic content features in Django Templates:
1.	Template Variables ({{ }})
2.	Template Tags ({% %} → loops, conditions)
3.	Template Inheritance (base layout)
________________________________________
 Step 1: Template Variables
In views.py we can pass values:
def homepage(request):
    context = {
        'username': 'Noureen',
        'age': 25
    }
    return render(request, 'blog/homepage.html', context)
blog/templates/blog/homepage.html
<h1>Welcome, {{ username }}!</h1>
<p>Your age is {{ age }}</p>
Output:
Welcome, Noureen!
Your age is 25
________________________________________
 Step 2: Template Tags (Loops & Conditions)
views.py
def student_list(request):
    students = ["Ali", "Riya", "John"]
    return render(request, 'blog/students.html', {'students': students})
students.html
<h2>Student List</h2>
<ul>
    {% for student in students %}
        <li>{{ student }}</li>
    {% empty %}
        <li>No students available</li>
    {% endfor %}
</ul>
 Output:
•	Ali
•	Riya
•	John
________________________________________
Conditions Example:
{% if students %}
    <p>Total students: {{ students|length }}</p>
{% else %}
    <p>No students found</p>
{% endif %}
________________________________________
 Step 3: Template Filters
Django provides filters to modify variables. Example:
<p>{{ username|upper }}</p>
<p>{{ username|length }}</p>
<p>{{ username|default:"Guest" }}</p>
 Output (if username = "Noureen")
•	NOUREEN
•	7
•	(shows "Guest" if username is empty)
________________________________________
 Step 4: Template Inheritance (Base Layout)
To avoid repeating HTML, Django allows base templates.
templates/base.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>{% block title %}My Site{% endblock %}</title>
</head>
<body>
    <header>
        <h1>My Website</h1>
        <hr>
    </header>

    <main>
        {% block content %}{% endblock %}
    </main>

    <footer>
        <hr>
        <p>Copyright © 2025</p>
    </footer>
</body>
</html>
blog/post_list.html
{% extends "base.html" %}

{% block title %}Posts{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<h2>Post List</h2>
<ul>
    {% for post in posts %}
        <li><a href="{% url 'post_detail' post.pk %}">{{ post.title }}</a></li>
    {% empty %}
        <li>No posts yet</li>
    {% endfor %}
</ul>
<a href="{% url 'add_post' %}">Add Post</a>
{% endblock %}
 Now all pages share the same header and footer.
________________________________________
 Step 5: Static Files (CSS, JS, Images)
Django handles static files like CSS/JS/images.
1.	Add in settings.py:
STATIC_URL = '/static/'
2.	Create folder → blog/static/blog/style.css
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    background-color: #f8f8f8;
}
h1, h2 { color: darkblue; }
3.	Load CSS in base.html:
{% load static %}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{% static 'blog/style.css' %}">
 Now all pages have a consistent look & feel.
________________________________________
 Summary for Topic 6
•	Learned template variables, tags, filters.
•	Created base layout with inheritance.
•	Used static files (CSS/JS/images).
•	Now app pages are dynamic and styled. 🎉
