Topic 7: Building Forms for Data Collection and Validation
So far we created a simple PostForm to add posts.
Now let’s explore Django Forms in detail:
1.	Forms in Django
2.	Collecting User Input
3.	Validating Data
4.	Displaying Form Errors
________________________________________
Step 1: Two Ways of Creating Forms
1.	Forms with forms.Form (manual fields)
2.	Forms with forms.ModelForm (auto fields from models)
We already used ModelForm. Let’s see both.
________________________________________
Example A: Simple Contact Form (forms.Form)
blog/forms.py
from django import forms

class ContactForm(forms.Form):
    name = forms.CharField(max_length=100, required=True)
    email = forms.EmailField(required=True)
    message = forms.CharField(widget=forms.Textarea, required=True)
________________________________________
blog/views.py
def contact_view(request):
    if request.method == "POST":
        form = ContactForm(request.POST)
        if form.is_valid():
            name = form.cleaned_data['name']
            email = form.cleaned_data['email']
            message = form.cleaned_data['message']
            # Here we could save to DB or send email
            return HttpResponse(f"Thanks {name}, we received your message!")
    else:
        form = ContactForm()
    return render(request, 'blog/contact.html', {'form': form})
________________________________________
blog/templates/blog/contact.html
<h1>Contact Us</h1>
<form method="post">
    {% csrf_token %}
    {{ form.as_p }}
    <button type="submit">Send</button>
</form>
________________________________________
Example B: Model Form (Auto from DB)
Already done with PostForm earlier. Django automatically maps model fields.
________________________________________
 Step 2: Form Validation
Django automatically validates fields based on type (e.g., EmailField, CharField).
We can also write custom validation.
forms.py
class ContactForm(forms.Form):
    name = forms.CharField(max_length=100, required=True)
    email = forms.EmailField(required=True)
    message = forms.CharField(widget=forms.Textarea, required=True)

    def clean_name(self):
        data = self.cleaned_data['name']
        if len(data) < 3:
            raise forms.ValidationError("Name must be at least 3 characters long")
        return data
 If user enters less than 3 chars → form shows an error.
________________________________________
 Step 3: Showing Errors in Template
contact.html
<h1>Contact Us</h1>
<form method="post">
    {% csrf_token %}
    {{ form.as_p }}

    {% if form.errors %}
        <div style="color:red;">
            <h4>Please fix the following errors:</h4>
            <ul>
                {% for field, errors in form.errors.items %}
                    <li>{{ field }}: {{ errors|join:", " }}</li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    <button type="submit">Send</button>
</form>
 Now validation errors are shown neatly.
________________________________________
 Step 4: Add URL
blog/urls.py
path('contact/', views.contact_view, name='contact'),
Visit → http://127.0.0.1:8000/contact/
________________________________________
 Step 5: Bonus – Styling Forms with Widgets
We can customize fields using widgets.
class ContactForm(forms.Form):
    name = forms.CharField(
        widget=forms.TextInput(attrs={'placeholder': 'Your Name', 'class': 'form-control'})
    )
    email = forms.EmailField(
        widget=forms.EmailInput(attrs={'placeholder': 'Your Email', 'class': 'form-control'})
    )
    message = forms.CharField(
        widget=forms.Textarea(attrs={'rows': 5, 'placeholder': 'Your Message'})
    )
 Now input boxes have placeholders and CSS classes for Bootstrap.
________________________________________
 Summary for Topic 7
•	Learned forms.Form (manual) & forms.ModelForm (auto from model).
•	Implemented validation (built-in + custom).
•	Displayed error messages in template.
•	Added widgets for styling.
