Topic 8: Django User Authentication and Authorization
Django comes with a ready-made authentication system that provides:
•	User login & logout
•	Password hashing
•	User signup & profile
•	Groups & permissions
________________________________________
 Step 1: Enable Authentication System
By default, Django includes authentication apps. Check mysite/settings.py →
INSTALLED_APPS = [
    'django.contrib.admin',
    'django.contrib.auth',        # Authentication
    'django.contrib.contenttypes',
    'django.contrib.sessions',
    'django.contrib.messages',
    'django.contrib.staticfiles',
    'blog',
]
 Already included.
________________________________________
Step 2: Create Signup (User Registration)
blog/forms.py
from django import forms
from django.contrib.auth.models import User
from django.contrib.auth.forms import UserCreationForm

class SignUpForm(UserCreationForm):
    email = forms.EmailField(required=True)

    class Meta:
        model = User
        fields = ['username', 'email', 'password1', 'password2']
________________________________________
blog/views.py
from django.contrib.auth import login
from .forms import SignUpForm

def signup_view(request):
    if request.method == "POST":
        form = SignUpForm(request.POST)
        if form.is_valid():
            user = form.save()
            login(request, user)  # auto login after signup
            return redirect('post_list')
    else:
        form = SignUpForm()
    return render(request, 'blog/signup.html', {'form': form})
________________________________________
blog/templates/blog/signup.html
<h1>Sign Up</h1>
<form method="post">
    {% csrf_token %}
    {{ form.as_p }}
    <button type="submit">Register</button>
</form>
________________________________________
 Step 3: User Login
Django provides a ready-made LoginView.
blog/views.py
from django.contrib.auth.views import LoginView

class CustomLoginView(LoginView):
    template_name = 'blog/login.html'
blog/templates/blog/login.html
<h1>Login</h1>
<form method="post">
    {% csrf_token %}
    {{ form.as_p }}
    <button type="submit">Login</button>
</form>
________________________________________
 Step 4: User Logout
Django provides LogoutView.
In mysite/urls.py add:
from django.contrib.auth.views import LogoutView
from blog.views import signup_view, CustomLoginView

urlpatterns = [
    path('admin/', admin.site.urls),
    path('signup/', signup_view, name='signup'),
    path('login/', CustomLoginView.as_view(), name='login'),
    path('logout/', LogoutView.as_view(next_page='login'), name='logout'),
    path('', include('blog.urls')),
]
________________________________________
 Step 5: Restrict Pages to Logged-in Users
We can protect views using @login_required.
blog/views.py
from django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required

@login_required
def post_list(request):
    posts = Post.objects.all()
    return render(request, 'blog/post_list.html', {'posts': posts})
 Now only logged-in users can see posts.
If not logged in → redirected to login page.
________________________________________
 Step 6: Update Settings for Login Redirects
In mysite/settings.py
LOGIN_URL = 'login'
LOGIN_REDIRECT_URL = 'post_list'
LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URL = 'login'
________________________________________
Step 7: Test Flow
1.	Visit → /signup/ → create new user
2.	Auto login → redirected to post list
3.	Logout → /logout/
4.	Try visiting /posts/ → redirected to /login/
5.	Login again → back to posts 🎉
________________________________________
 Summary for Topic 8
•	Used Django’s built-in auth system
•	Implemented signup, login, logout
•	Restricted views using @login_required
•	Set redirects for login/logout
________________________________________
7. Building Forms for Data Collection and Validation
Django provides powerful Forms to handle user input, validation, and saving data.
________________________________________
Step 1: Why Forms in Django?
•	HTML forms collect input, but validating and handling them manually is time-consuming.
•	Django’s Form class helps with:
o	Rendering HTML automatically
o	Validating input (e.g., email format, required fields)
o	Converting data into Python types
o	Saving directly to the database (with ModelForm)
________________________________________
Step 2: Create a Form
Let’s create a Contact Form where users can submit their name, email, and message.
 File structure (new files in bold):
myproject/
    myapp/
        forms.py      <-- NEW
        views.py
        templates/
            contact.html
________________________________________
forms.py
from django import forms

class ContactForm(forms.Form):
    name = forms.CharField(max_length=100, required=True, label="Your Name")
    email = forms.EmailField(required=True, label="Email Address")
    message = forms.CharField(widget=forms.Textarea, required=True, label="Message")
________________________________________
views.py
from django.shortcuts import render
from .forms import ContactForm

def contact_view(request):
    if request.method == "POST":
        form = ContactForm(request.POST)  # bind data
        if form.is_valid():
            # process cleaned data
            name = form.cleaned_data['name']
            email = form.cleaned_data['email']
            message = form.cleaned_data['message']

            # Here you can save to DB or send email
            print(f"Received message from {name} ({email}): {message}")

            return render(request, "success.html", {"name": name})
    else:
        form = ContactForm()  # empty form

    return render(request, "contact.html", {"form": form})
________________________________________
contact.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Contact Form</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>Contact Us</h2>
    <form method="post">
        {% csrf_token %}
        {{ form.as_p }}
        <button type="submit">Send</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
________________________________________
success.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Success</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>Thank you, {{ name }}! Your message was sent successfully.</h2>
</body>
</html>
________________________________________
Step 3: Add URL
# myapp/urls.py
from django.urls import path
from .views import contact_view

urlpatterns = [
    path("contact/", contact_view, name="contact"),
]
Include it in project urls.py if not already:
from django.urls import include, path

urlpatterns = [
    path('', include('myapp.urls')),
]
________________________________________
Step 4: Run and Test
python manage.py runserver
Go to  http://127.0.0.1:8000/contact/
•	Try submitting an empty form → Django will show validation errors.
•	Try entering valid data → You’ll see success page.
________________________________________
 Now you’ve learned how to build forms for data collection and validation in Django.
________________________________________
6. Django Views, Templates, and Forms
In Django, the View is responsible for handling requests and returning responses.
The Template is used for rendering HTML dynamically.
The Form is used for collecting and validating user input.
________________________________________
Step 1: Create a View
Open your app (myapp/views.py) and add:
from django.shortcuts import render
from django.http import HttpResponse

def home_view(request):
    return HttpResponse("Welcome to Django Home Page")

def about_view(request):
    return render(request, 'about.html')
________________________________________
Step 2: Add URL Patterns
In myapp/urls.py:
from django.urls import path
from . import views

urlpatterns = [
    path('', views.home_view, name='home'),
    path('about/', views.about_view, name='about'),
]
Include app URLs inside myproject/urls.py:
from django.contrib import admin
from django.urls import path, include

urlpatterns = [
    path('admin/', admin.site.urls),
    path('', include('myapp.urls')),
]
________________________________________
Step 3: Create Templates
Inside myapp/templates/ folder, create about.html:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>About Page</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>About Django Project</h1>
    <p>This is the about page rendered using a template.</p>
</body>
</html>
________________________________________
Step 4: Using Forms in Django
Create a form in myapp/forms.py:
from django import forms

class ContactForm(forms.Form):
    name = forms.CharField(max_length=100)
    email = forms.EmailField()
    message = forms.CharField(widget=forms.Textarea)
________________________________________
Step 5: Create a View for Form
In views.py:
from django.shortcuts import render
from .forms import ContactForm

def contact_view(request):
    form = ContactForm()
    if request.method == 'POST':
        form = ContactForm(request.POST)
        if form.is_valid():
            # Process the data
            name = form.cleaned_data['name']
            email = form.cleaned_data['email']
            message = form.cleaned_data['message']
            return render(request, 'success.html', {'name': name})
    return render(request, 'contact.html', {'form': form})
________________________________________
Step 6: Create Templates for Form
Inside templates/contact.html:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Contact</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Contact Form</h1>
    <form method="post">
        {% csrf_token %}
        {{ form.as_p }}
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
Inside templates/success.html:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Success</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Thank you {{ name }}! Your message has been submitted.</h1>
</body>
</html>
________________________________________
Now you can test:
•	Visit http://127.0.0.1:8000/ → Home page.
•	Visit http://127.0.0.1:8000/about/ → Template rendered.
•	Visit http://127.0.0.1:8000/contact/ → Contact form.
________________________________________
Working with Django Templates for Dynamic Content
What are Django Templates?
Django templates are files (usually .html) where you define how your website pages should look.
They allow you to write dynamic content by mixing HTML with Django Template Language (DTL).
 Instead of hardcoding everything in HTML, you can pass data from views and display it dynamically in templates.
________________________________________
 File Structure (example)
myproject/
│── myapp/
│   │── views.py
│   │── urls.py
│   │── models.py
│   │── templates/
│   │     └── myapp/
│   │           └── home.html
│
│── myproject/
│   │── settings.py
│   │── urls.py
________________________________________
 Step 1: Update settings.py
Make sure Django knows where your templates are located.
# settings.py
TEMPLATES = [
    {
        'BACKEND': 'django.template.backends.django.DjangoTemplates',
        'DIRS': [BASE_DIR / "templates"],  # Global templates folder
        'APP_DIRS': True,
        'OPTIONS': {
            'context_processors': [
                'django.template.context_processors.debug',
                'django.template.context_processors.request',
                'django.contrib.auth.context_processors.auth',
                'django.contrib.messages.context_processors.messages',
            ],
        },
    },
]
________________________________________
 Step 2: Create a Template
Create a file:
myapp/templates/myapp/home.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Django Template Example</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome, {{ user_name }}!</h1>
    <p>Today's date is: {{ today }}</p>

    <h2>Fruits List:</h2>
    <ul>
        {% for fruit in fruits %}
            <li>{{ fruit }}</li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ul>

    <p>{% if is_logged_in %} You are logged in! {% else %} Please log in. {% endif %}</p>
</body>
</html>
________________________________________
 Step 3: Update Views
Pass data from the view to the template.
# myapp/views.py
from django.shortcuts import render
from datetime import date

def home(request):
    context = {
        "user_name": "Noureen",
        "today": date.today(),
        "fruits": ["Apple", "Mango", "Banana"],
        "is_logged_in": True
    }
    return render(request, "myapp/home.html", context)
________________________________________
 Step 4: Map URL
# myapp/urls.py
from django.urls import path
from . import views

urlpatterns = [
    path("", views.home, name="home"),
]
Also include in project-level urls.py:
# myproject/urls.py
from django.contrib import admin
from django.urls import path, include

urlpatterns = [
    path('admin/', admin.site.urls),
    path('', include('myapp.urls')),
]
________________________________________
 Step 5: Run the Server
python manage.py runserver
Open → http://127.0.0.1:8000/
 You will see a dynamic page with:
•	A welcome message using your name
•	Today’s date
•	A list of fruits from the view
•	A conditional login message
________________________________________
 Key Features of Django Templates:
•	{{ variable }} → display variables
•	{% for %} {% endfor %} → loops
•	{% if %} {% else %} {% endif %} → conditions
•	Template inheritance using {% extends %} and {% block %} (for layouts like headers/footers)
Topic 12: Django User Authentication and Authorization
Now that we’ve handled forms, let’s move into User Authentication (login/logout) and Authorization (permissions, restricting access).
________________________________________
 Step 1: What is Authentication vs Authorization?
•	Authentication → Verifying who the user is (login, signup).
•	Authorization → Checking what the user can do (permissions, staff-only access).
________________________________________
 Step 2: Default Django Authentication System
Django provides:
•	User model (django.contrib.auth.models.User)
•	Authentication views (LoginView, LogoutView)
•	Middleware for session handling
•	Permissions & groups
________________________________________
 Step 3: Enable Authentication
In settings.py, make sure these are present:
INSTALLED_APPS = [
    ...
    'django.contrib.auth',
    'django.contrib.contenttypes',
    'django.contrib.sessions',
    'django.contrib.messages',
    'django.contrib.staticfiles',
]
Also enable middleware:
MIDDLEWARE = [
    ...
    'django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware',
    'django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware',
    'django.contrib.messages.middleware.MessageMiddleware',
]
________________________________________
 Step 4: Create Superuser
Run:
python manage.py createsuperuser
 Enter username, email, and password.
Now you can log in at: http://127.0.0.1:8000/admin/
________________________________________
 Step 5: User Login & Logout
urls.py
from django.urls import path
from django.contrib.auth import views as auth_views

urlpatterns = [
    path('login/', auth_views.LoginView.as_view(template_name="login.html"), name="login"),
    path('logout/', auth_views.LogoutView.as_view(next_page="/"), name="logout"),
]
login.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Login</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>User Login</h2>
    <form method="post">
        {% csrf_token %}
        {{ form.as_p }}
        <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
Protected View Example
from django.shortcuts import render
from django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required

@login_required
def dashboard(request):
    return render(request, "dashboard.html")
Now only logged-in users can see the dashboard.
________________________________________
Step 6: Authorization (Permissions & Groups)
Django provides built-in permissions like add, change, delete, view.
You can also define custom permissions inside your model.
Example: Restrict staff-only page
from django.contrib.admin.views.decorators import staff_member_required

@staff_member_required
def staff_page(request):
    return render(request, "staff.html")
Example: Check specific permission
from django.contrib.auth.decorators import permission_required

@permission_required('app_name.can_publish')
def publish_post(request):
    return render(request, "publish.html")
________________________________________
 File Structure After Authentication
mysite/
│── manage.py
│── mysite/
│   └── settings.py
│   └── urls.py
│── myapp/
│   └── views.py
│── templates/
│   └── login.html
│   └── dashboard.html
│   └── staff.html
________________________________________
 With this, you now have Login, Logout, and restricted access views set up in Django.
Creating and Handling Views for User Interactions in Django
________________________________________
What are Views in Django?
•	A view in Django is just a Python function or class that takes a web request and returns a web response.
•	Views are the brain of your app – they process data, interact with models, and render templates.
________________________________________
Example Project Context
We’ll extend our mysite project and myapp app that we already created.
We’ll add a few views to handle user interactions.
________________________________________
Steps
1. Open myapp/views.py
This is where you define your functions.
Example:
from django.http import HttpResponse
from django.shortcuts import render

# Simple text response
def home(request):
    return HttpResponse("Hello! Welcome to the Home Page.")

# Using template
def about(request):
    return render(request, "about.html", {"title": "About Us"})
________________________________________
2. Connect Views with URLs
In myapp/urls.py:
from django.urls import path
from . import views

urlpatterns = [
    path("", views.home, name="home"),
    path("about/", views.about, name="about"),
]
And in mysite/urls.py include app URLs:
from django.contrib import admin
from django.urls import path, include

urlpatterns = [
    path("admin/", admin.site.urls),
    path("", include("myapp.urls")),
]
________________________________________
3. Create a Template
Inside myapp/templates/, create about.html:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>{{ title }}</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    <p>This is the About Page.</p>
</body>
</html>
________________________________________
4. Run the Server
python manage.py runserver
Visit:
•	http://127.0.0.1:8000/ → Shows "Hello! Welcome to the Home Page."
•	http://127.0.0.1:8000/about/ → Renders the template with dynamic content.
________________________________________
 You just created and handled views in Django!
•	home → returns direct text response
•	about → returns an HTML template with dynamic data
________________________________________
Topic 14: Introduction to Flask Microframework
Now that we’ve gone through Django concepts, let’s shift to Flask, which is a lightweight Python web framework.
________________________________________
 What is Flask?
•	Flask is a micro web framework for Python.
•	Unlike Django (which is full-stack), Flask is minimalistic and gives developers the freedom to choose their tools.
•	It is commonly used for building small to medium web applications and REST APIs.
________________________________________
 Flask Features:
•	Lightweight and minimal.
•	Built-in development server.
•	Supports URL routing.
•	Jinja2 template engine.
•	Easy to integrate with databases (SQLite, MySQL, PostgreSQL).
•	Extensible with many Flask extensions.
________________________________________
Installation
First, make sure you have Python installed. Then install Flask:
pip install flask
Verify installation:
pip show flask
________________________________________
 File Structure for a Basic Flask App
flask_project/
│
├── app.py
└── templates/
    └── index.html
•	app.py → main application file.
•	templates/ → folder for HTML templates.
________________________________________
Example 1: Hello World in Flask
app.py
from flask import Flask

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/")
def home():
    return "Hello, Flask!"

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(debug=True)
 Run with:
python app.py
Visit: http://127.0.0.1:5000/
________________________________________
 Example 2: Rendering HTML Template
app.py
from flask import Flask, render_template

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/")
def home():
    return render_template("index.html")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(debug=True)
templates/index.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Flask Intro</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to Flask!</h1>
</body>
</html>
 Now Flask renders HTML using Jinja2 templates.
________________________________________
1. Introduction to Flask Microframework
👉 Example:
Create a simple Flask app that returns "Hello, Flask!" when you open http://127.0.0.1:5000/ in the browser.
________________________________________
2. Setting Up a Flask Project
👉 Example:
Set up a new Flask project with the following structure:
/flask_app  
   ├── app.py  
   ├── templates/  
   ├── static/  
   └── requirements.txt  
Run the app and make sure it works at http://localhost:5000/.
________________________________________
3. Introduction to Python Hyper HTTP-2 Framework (Hypercorn / HTTPX)
Example:
Run your Flask app with Hypercorn (instead of the default Flask server) to support HTTP/2:
hypercorn app:app --bind 127.0.0.1:5000 --reload
________________________________________
4. Defining Routes and Handling Requests
Example:
Create two routes:
•	/hello/<name> → Returns "Hello <name>!"
•	/square/<int:num> → Returns the square of the number
________________________________________
5. Flask Blueprints and Extensions
 Example:
•	Create a users blueprint with a route /users/profile
•	Register the blueprint in app.py
•	Use an extension like Flask-Mail to send a welcome email.
________________________________________
6. Organizing Code with Blueprints
 Example:
Organize your project like this:
/flask_app  
   ├── app.py  
   ├── users/  
   │     ├── __init__.py  
   │     ├── routes.py  
   └── products/  
         ├── __init__.py  
         ├── routes.py  
Each blueprint (users, products) should have its own routes, then register both inside app.py.

